  faqs={[
    {q:"Why is Santiago so cheap compared to other Panamanian cities?", a:"Santiago has not been 'discovered' by the expat community. There is minimal tourism and almost no international community — which means local pricing prevails. A 2-bedroom furnished apartment rents for $275–$400/month in documented cases. Meals at local restaurants cost $4–6. Services have no markup for foreigners because foreigners are rarely there."},
    {q:"Is Santiago suitable for remote workers?", a:"Increasingly yes. Fiber internet has arrived in urban Santiago, and speeds are competitive with other Panamanian cities. The new Multi Mall and commercial center provide air-conditioned coworking-style spaces. The main challenge is the lack of English-speaking community — social life for remote workers will primarily be online or involve travel to larger expat hubs."},
    {q:"What is there to do near Santiago?", a:"The Pacific coast is about 60–90 minutes west, with surf beaches and fishing communities. Coiba Island National Park — a UNESCO World Heritage Site with world-class diving — is accessible as a day trip from the coast. The Santa Fe highlands (cooler mountain town) are about 45 minutes north. It's a hub for exploring Veraguas province."},
    {q:"Is Santiago's healthcare adequate?", a:"Santiago has a regional public hospital and private clinics. For routine care, it's adequate. Complex specialties and serious emergencies are typically handled in Panama City (3 hours) or David (3 hours). Expats in Santiago typically maintain a local insurance plan and budget for occasional travel to the capital for specialized care."},
  ]}
